1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,660
SUSIE: So Luke, for our next psychology assignment

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,580
we have to do something on sleep and dreams.

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:16,620
LUKE: Right. I've just read an article suggesting why we tend to forget most of our dreams soon after we wake up.

4
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,202
I mean, most of my dreams aren't that interesting anyway,

5
00:00:20,202 --> 00:00:23,784
but what it said was that if we remembered everything,

6
00:00:23,784 --> 00:00:28,440
we might get mixed up about what actually happened and what we dreamed.

7
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:31,140
So it's a sort of protection.

8
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,540
I hadn't heard that idea before.

9
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:39,160
I'd always assumed that it was just that we didn't have room in our memories for all that stuff.

10
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:45,300
SUSIE: Me too. What do you think about the idea that our dreams may predict the future?

11
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:47,820
LUKE: It's a belief that you get all over the world.

12
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:52,548
SUSIE: Yeah, lots of people have a story of it happening to them,

13
00:00:52,548 --> 00:00:57,275
but the explanation I've read is that for each dream that comes true,

14
00:00:57,275 --> 00:00:59,120
we have thousands that don't

15
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:02,730
but we don't notice those, we don't even remember them.

16
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:06,948
We just remember the ones where something in the real world,

17
00:01:06,948 --> 00:01:11,550
like a view or an action, happens to trigger a dream memory.

18
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:15,230
LUKE: Right. So it's just a coincidence really.

19
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:20,340
Something else I read about is what they call segmented sleeping.

20
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,752
That's a theory that hundreds of years ago,

21
00:01:22,752 --> 00:01:26,000
people used to get up in the middle of the night

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,990
and have a chat or something to eat, then go back to bed.

23
00:01:29,990 --> 00:01:31,790
So I tried it myself.

24
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:32,890
SUSIE: Why?

25
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:35,430
LUKE: Well it's meant to make you more creative.

26
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:39,020
I don't know why. But I gave it up after a week.

27
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,820
It just didn't fit in with my lifestyle.

28
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:46,890
SUSIE: But most pre-school children have a short sleep in the day don't they?

29
00:01:46,890 --> 00:01:54,080
There was an experiment some students did here last term to see at what age kids should stop having naps.

30
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,840
But they didn't really find an answer.

31
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:01,073
They spent a lot of time working out the most appropriate methodology,

32
00:02:01,073 --> 00:02:04,600
but the results didn't seem to show any obvious patterns.

33
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:08,960
LUKE: Right. Anyway, let's think about our assignment.

34
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,282
Last time I had problems with the final stage,

35
00:02:12,282 --> 00:02:17,080
where we had to describe and justify how successful we thought we'd been.

36
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,150
I struggled a bit with the action plan too.

37
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:22,300
SUSIE: I was OK with the planning

38
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,830
but I got marked down for the self-assessment as well.

39
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:31,820
And I had big problems with the statistical stuff, that's where I really lost marks.

40
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:34,370
LUKE: Right.

41
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:39,290
SUSIE: So shall we plan what we have to do for this assignment?

42
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:40,470
LUKE: OK.

43
00:02:40,470 --> 00:02:43,700
SUSIE: First, we have to decide on our research question.

44
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:52,610
So how about 'Is there a relationship between hours of sleep and number of dreams?'

45
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:59,630
LUKE: OK. Then we need to think about who we'll do the study on. About 12 people?

46
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:04,100
SUSIE: Right. And shall we use other psychology students?

47
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:09,520
LUKE: Let's use people from a different department. What about history?

48
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:15,050
SUSIE: Yes, they might have interesting dreams! Or literature students?

49
00:03:15,050 --> 00:03:16,730
LUKE: I don't really know any.

50
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:22,350
SUSIE: OK, forget that idea. Then we have to think about our methodology.

51
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:27,660
So we could use observation, but that doesn't seem appropriate.

52
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:31,560
LUKE: No. It needs to be self-reporting I think

53
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:34,820
And we could ask them to answer questions online.

54
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:36,898
SUSIE: But in this case,

55
00:03:36,898 --> 00:03:42,160
paper might be better as they'll be doing it straight after they wake up

56
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:45,210
in fact while they're still half-asleep.

57
00:03:45,210 --> 00:03:50,490
LUKE: Right. And we'll have to check the ethical guidelines for this sort of research.

58
00:03:50,490 --> 00:03:56,750
SUSIE: Mm, because our experiment involves humans, so there are special regulations.

59
00:03:56,750 --> 00:04:01,370
LUKE: Yes, I had a look at those for another assignment I did.

60
00:04:01,370 --> 00:04:04,418
There's a whole section on risk assessment,

61
00:04:04,418 --> 00:04:10,080
and another section on making sure they aren't put under any unnecessary stress.

62
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:12,970
SUSIE: Let's hope they don't have any bad dreams!

63
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:14,640
LUKE: Yeah.

64
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:20,430
SUSIE: Then when we've collected all our data we have to analyse it

65
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:23,994
and calculate the correlation between our two variables,

66
00:04:23,994 --> 00:04:27,620
that's time sleeping and number of dreams

67
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,120
and then present our results visually in a graph.

68
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:36,060
LUKE: Right. And the final thing is to think about our research and evaluate it.

69
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:38,930
So that seems quite straightforward.

70
00:04:38,930 --> 00:04:42,930
SUSIE: Yeah. So now let's...
